/api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/I-am-nothing","id":56163905,"login":"I-am-nothing","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2MTYzOTA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commits/67541494f18128c69198b2b56feed30f79f246e5/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-27T07:19:01Z","email":"56163905+I-am-nothing@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Sheng Kun Chang"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-27T07:19:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(sdk): nine defects — body caps, MCP schemas, arg validation, annotations, httpx, comment rot (#189)\n\n* fix(core): cap every route''s body, stop leaking import paths into MCP schemas\n\nFour defects that share one shape — a rule written for one case and never\nextended to the sibling case that needed it more.\n\n**The body cap protected the wrong surface.** `scopedRoutes` applied\n`MaxBytes` only when `scope == ScopeInternal`. Internal routes require a shared\nsecret and are reachable only from the platform; Public routes are anonymous\nand are the one surface a stranger can post to. The guarded scope was capped\nand the unguarded one was not — an anonymous POST could stream until the\nmodule ran out of memory. Verified: a 1 MB + 1 byte POST to `/public/*`\nreturned 200 with the whole body read; it is now 413. API Gateway''s 6 MB gave\nproduction a ceiling by accident, but `mirrorstack dev` had none.\n\nNo per-module override, deliberately: nothing in the fleet reads a multipart\nbody or a file upload on any route — bulk bytes go through presigned storage\nURLs and never touch a module route.\n\n**Every MCP tool schema shipped the SDK''s package layout.** `deriveMCPSchema`\nlacked the `Anonymous` its sibling `derivePayloadSchema` has always had, so\neach tool''s inputSchema carried\n\n    \"$id\":\"https://github.com/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/internal/core/args\"\n\nplus a `$schema` dialect line no MCP client reads. A tools/list is re-sent\nevery turn, so both were paid per turn, per tool, forever. The derived schema\ndrops from 245 to 129 bytes on a one-field struct; `type:\"object\"` and the\ninline shape the MCP spec requires are asserted in the test.\n\n**`examples/template` was never compiled.** It is its own Go module, so\n`./...` never reached it — which is how it came to ship a `Config` that omits\n`Slug` entirely, teaching a shape the catalog will not accept. CI now builds it\nagainst this tree via its own `replace`, so an SDK change that breaks the\ndocumented example fails here instead of in a reader''s terminal. The docs\ncalled it \"the working scaffold the CLI pulls from\" in both locales; the CLI\nvendors its own templates and does not read this at all.\n\n**`MCPResource` cannot be reached by anything.** Dispatch answers\n`method not found` to every `resources/*` call — its v1 capability set is tools\nonly, by design — and the SDK advertises no resources capability either.\nAdoption is 0 of 11 modules, which is what a surface with no client looks like.\nMarked `Deprecated:` on both the public and internal declarations, pointing at\nan argument-less `MCPTool` as the reachable equivalent. Not removed: that\nbreaks a published API, and there is no evidence anyone calls it — only\nevidence that it would not work if they did.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): enforce the input schema the module publishes, and name undocumented fields\n\nMCPTool''s doc comment has always promised arguments are \"validated against\nincoming JSON at call time\". Nothing validated anything: the call path ran\njson.Unmarshal, which ignores both constraints the derived schema states.\n\n**`required` was advertised and enforced by nobody.** Go leaves an absent field\nat its zero value, so a tool declaring a required `userId` ran with `userId:\"\"`\n— and if the handler read that as \"all users\" or \"me\", it did the wrong thing\nand said nothing. An LLM omitting an argument is the ordinary case, not the\nexotic one.\n\n**`additionalProperties:false` likewise.** A model calling `{\"user_id\": …}`\nagainst a `userId` field got the silent zero value AND no signal it had\nmisspelled anything. Both now return ErrInvalidArgs, which the MCP layer turns\ninto an in-band error the model can read and retry against.\n\nPresence is checked against the raw object rather than the decoded struct,\nbecause JSON Schema''s `required` is about the KEY: an explicit null satisfies\nit, and a zero value from an absent key does not. DisallowUnknownFields is\nexactly additionalProperties:false here — the schema''s properties are derived\nfrom the struct''s fields, so the two sets are the same at every nesting level.\nThe required list is parsed once at registration, not per call.\n\n**0 of 45 shipped tools documented a single field**, and the reason turned out\nto be that nothing ever mentioned it was possible: `jsonschema:\"description=…\"`\nstruct tags already flow into the derived schema at zero cost. Registration now\nlogs a warning naming every undescribed input field, sorted so the line is\nstable across boots, and the doc comment shows the tag. A warning, not an error\n— failing a module''s boot over prose is a worse trade than shipping it\nundocumented.\n\nNot included: ToolReadOnly/ToolDestructive/ToolIdempotent annotations. The\ndispatch MCP surface carries no annotations field at all, so declaring them\nhere would stop at the manifest and be read by nobody — the same\ndeclared-but-unimplemented shape this commit is removing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(docs): cite symbols in comments, never line numbers\n\nThis repo carried 19 cross-file citations of the form `db.go:103`. Resolving\neach one against the file it names showed **14 of them pointed at the wrong\nthing**: a blank line, a fragment of an unrelated comment, a closing brace, a\nlogger call inside a different function. They had drifted by 1 to 92 lines\nwhile still reading as authoritative.\n\n    cited                          actually lands on\n    call.go:29                     (blank line)            — meant callHTTP, at :63\n    module.go:196                  m.logger.Printf(...)    — meant moduleIDPattern, at :208\n    system/manifest.go:209         return ui               — meant manifestHandler, at :301\n    select.go:98                   }                       — meant queryDynamicSelect\n    module.go:168                  // schema identifier.   — meant devAppSchemaMiddleware, at :180\n\nA reference that is confidently wrong is worse than no reference: it sends the\nnext reader somewhere real and irrelevant. Nothing about editing the cited file\nmakes the citing file''s comment update, so this rots by construction.\n\nAll 19 now name a symbol. `resolvePoolFor (db.go)` costs one grep and cannot\ndrift; `db.go:103` was already three lines stale the day it was written.\n\nA test enforces it repo-wide, in `go test ./...` rather than CI-only so it\nfails before the push. It scans comments in every .go file including tests —\na rotted comment misleads just as well in a test — and it first asserts its own\nmatcher fires on `db.go:103`, `select.go:120-140` and `system/manifest.go:209`\nand does NOT fire on `resolvePoolFor (db.go)`. Without that, a regression\nmaking the pattern match nothing would read as a pass.\n\nVerified by planting `see db.go:103` and watching it fail:\n\n    comment_citations_test.go:92: comment cites a line number:\n      internal/core/db.go:296 cites db.go:103 — name the symbol instead\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(mcp): ToolReadOnly / ToolDestructive / ToolIdempotent / ToolTitle\n\nThe variadic MCPToolOption seam was built for exactly this, and the only reason\nit stayed empty is that there was nowhere for the value to go — nothing between\nthe module and the model carried an annotations field, so declaring these would\nhave produced data read by nobody.\n\napi-platform now carries them end to end (migration 075 → the MCP catalog →\nboth wire shapes → the agent''s tool description), so these are real the day\nthey ship. A tool marked ToolDestructive reaches the model as:\n\n    [DESTRUCTIVE: this changes or removes data irreversibly]\n    Delete a user.\n    Returns: {deleted: boolean}\n\n**Unset is not read-only, and this is the whole design.** The hints are\n*pointers* internally and omitted entirely when undeclared, because absent\nmeans \"the module said nothing\" while false means \"the module said no\" — a\nreader that cannot tell those apart has lost the only thing a hint is for.\nDefaulting readOnly to true would silently vouch for every tool ever written,\nincluding the ones that delete things, which is exactly backwards for a hint\nwhose only job is to let a caller relax.\n\nNothing is emitted at all when no option is given: an empty `{}` would read\ndownstream as a module that considered the question and declined, which is a\ndifferent claim from never having been asked.\n\nThe object is marshalled from a map, so keys are sorted and the manifest bytes\nare stable across boots — a manifest that reshuffled would look like a content\nchange to every consumer that hashes it. A test pins that two callers passing\nthe same options in different order produce identical bytes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(httpx): one pagination shape for every module\n\nAcross the shipped modules there were three pagination dialects and two\nBYTE-IDENTICAL cursor functions written independently in different repos:\ncredit''s `auditCursor` and users-roles'' `roleAuditCursor` are the same function\ncharacter for character, and user-core runs a third scheme on top. Nothing was\nwrong with any one of them — there was simply no owner for the convention, so\nevery module invented it again. This package is that owner.\n\n**Keyset always, offset never.** There is no Offset here and there will not be\none. Offset pagination is wrong under concurrent writes in a way that never\nshows up in testing: a row inserted before the cursor position shifts every\nlater row down, so page 2 re-shows a row from page 1, and a delete makes page 2\nskip one. Audit logs and ledgers — most of what modules paginate — are exactly\nthe append-heavy tables where that happens constantly.\n\n**The cursor is opaque**, base64url over an internal form. A cursor a client\ncan read is a cursor a client will parse, and then the encoding cannot change\nwithout breaking them.\n\nThree decisions the tests pin, because each was a real bug in one of the\ndialects being replaced:\n\n  - the (time, id) cursor keeps NANOSECONDS. A second-resolution cursor on a\n    table that writes faster than that loses rows at every page boundary, and\n    the id half is the tiebreak that makes the keyset total — two rows written\n    in the same instant otherwise make a boundary ambiguous.\n  - the separator is 0x1f, not ''|''. It cannot occur in an RFC3339 timestamp or\n    a UUID, so a value carrying one cannot split the cursor in the wrong place.\n  - an undecodable cursor is the FIRST page, not a 400. Same answer as no\n    cursor, cannot leak whether some other row exists, and a stale cursor from\n    an old client degrades to \"start over\" rather than an error nobody can act\n    on.\n\nPage[T] marshals an empty page as `{\"items\":[]}` and never null, and\nNextCursor is nil rather than \"\" on the last page — so \"no more\" and \"a\nboundary that encoded as empty\" cannot be confused. Truncated/Note describe a\npage whose ITEMS were cut, which is a different fact from having successors;\nboth can be true at once.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: write down the module skeleton all 11 modules already use\n\nThe rule existed and was universally followed — every shipped module has both\n`declare/` and `internal/handlers/` — but it was written down in exactly one\nplace: one module''s own CLAUDE.md. A new author had to reverse-engineer it from\na neighbour, and `mirrorstack module init` scaffolded a different pattern\nentirely (a postInitHooks registry with 0 of 11 adoption).\n\nThe boundary is one question: does this code mount a route or carry an\nhttp.HandlerFunc? No → `declare/`. Yes → `internal/handlers/`.\n\nThat split is not aesthetic. The declaration set BECOMES the manifest, and the\nmanifest is what the platform reads to decide what a module may do — install\npreconditions, permission defaults, exposed tables, contribution slots. Keeping\nit in files that cannot see a request is what makes it a statement about the\nmodule rather than about one caller.\n\nTwo rules recorded because both were learned the hard way:\n\n  - main.go never grows. Two Register() entry points, not a pile of init()\n    functions — an init() registry looks tidier and costs deterministic\n    ordering, and i18n catalogs MUST load before permission labels resolve.\n  - never name a file handlers.go. It is the name you reach for when you have\n    not decided what the code is, and it is how one module''s handlers.go\n    reached 1038 lines holding sessions, admin, profile and metering at once.\n\nBoth locales, linked from both READMEs. This is what the CLI scaffold should\nproduce (D6) — it does not yet.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sdk): unblock the two things standing in front of any latency fix\n\nBoth are one-line changes that do nothing on their own and are load-bearing the\nmoment anyone parallelises a module''s contribution fan-out.\n\n**cache.ForApp panicked on every real app id.** Its idPattern was\n`^[a-z0-9_]+$` — no hyphens — but app ids are UUIDs. runtime.AppSchemaName\nexists precisely because of it, rewriting ''-'' to ''_'' before the value can be a\nPostgres identifier; nothing did the equivalent here. So the first module to\ncall ms.Cache().ForApp(ms.AppID(ctx), …) crashed on its first real request, and\nthe unit test passed because it used a hand-written \"app_abc123\" no caller\nwould ever produce.\n\nThe only thing that pattern has to stop is a COLON — keys are\n{appID}:{moduleID}:{key}, so an id carrying '':'' could climb out of its own\nnamespace and read another app''s data. A hyphen cannot break a prefix boundary.\nExcluding it bought nothing and cost the whole cache. Colons, spaces and\nuppercase still panic, and a test pins that.\n\n**callHTTP inherited DefaultTransport''s 2-connection idle pool.** Every\ninter-module call from a module goes to the SAME host (dispatch). While a\nfan-out is serial that is invisible — concurrency never exceeds 1. The moment a\nhost module fans out to N contributors in parallel, connections 3..N cannot be\npooled: each pays a fresh TCP handshake (plus TLS in production) and is CLOSED\nrather than returned, on every request. Most of the parallel win goes straight\nback into handshakes, and it presents as \"parallelising didn''t help much\"\nrather than as a misconfigured pool.\n\nNow an explicit transport CLONED from DefaultTransport — proxy handling,\ntimeouts and HTTP/2 unchanged, only the pool sizes raised. A nil clone (if\nsomething replaced DefaultTransport) falls back to the old behaviour rather\nthan guessing.\n\nThe test asserts it here because the symptom appears in a different repo: the\nmodule doing the fan-out pays for it, the SDK causes it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(core): split the SDK''s own 1069-line module.go\n\nmodule.go held config validation, construction, all three route scopes, the\npermission system, startup and shutdown, the reserved /__mirrorstack/* route\ntree, the package-level default module, and contribution slots.\n\nIt is the SDK''s own version of the catch-all this codebase now tells module\nauthors not to write — the same shape as the user-core handlers.go split in\nms-app-modules #265, in the repo that documents the rule.\n\n    module.go                  141   construction, and the accessors others reach through\n    module_config.go           225   declared identity + construction-time validation\n    module_scopes.go           148   the three scopes and each one''s middleware chain\n    module_permissions.go      186   declaration, qualification, enforcement, i18n labels\n    module_start.go            180   serving mode, the one-shot task path, Close\n    module_system_routes.go    148   the reserved /__mirrorstack/* namespace\n    module_default.go           57   the default module and the ms.* wrappers\n    module_contributions.go    127   declaring slots, reading what others registered\n\nPure motion — no logic changed, `go test -race ./...` passes unchanged, and\nimports were pruned per file by goimports rather than by hand.\n\nThe `module_` prefix rather than bare names (config.go, start.go) because this\npackage already has 30 files and a bare `start.go` would read as the package''s\nstartup rather than the Module type''s.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct the declare/ rule — event and cron handlers are a real exception\n\nThe rule as written said nothing in declare/ should reference an *http.Request.\nWriting the enforcement script disproved it: ms.OnEvent and ms.Cron take a\nhandler with an HTTP shape, and every module using them keeps that handler in\ndeclare/ beside its subscription. Moving it would need declare/ to import\nhandlers, which already imports declare — so the honest options are an import\ncycle or a third package, and the fleet had settled this correctly.\n\nFlagging those was a false positive on a pattern that was already right. What\ndeclare/ must never do is MOUNT A ROUTE, and that is what\nscripts/verify-module.sh now checks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(httpx): Cap — bound a tool result by dropping whole items, never bytes\n\n🔴 The obvious implementation is the broken one. Marshalling a result and\ncutting the string is how most size caps get written, and it produces INVALID\nJSON: the reader gets a parse error instead of data, or — worse — a\nhalf-written object it silently mis-reads. Every test here asserts the result\nstill parses, at every size, including when nothing fit.\n\nCap drops whole items until the marshalled slice fits, and returns a page that\nSAYS SO: Truncated marks it, Note gives the count and how to recover the rest,\nand NextCursor is the position to resume from. A model reading it asks for the\nnext page instead of concluding the list is short — which is exactly what a\nsilently-shortened list teaches it to do.\n\nAn empty result is marked truncated too, rather than being an empty list the\ncaller reads as \"there is nothing\".\n\nIt halves rather than steps, so a single oversized item costs log(n) marshals\ninstead of n marshals of an already-too-large slice. An unmarshalable item is\nreturned as-is: that is the caller''s bug, and failing where it can be diagnosed\nbeats silently returning an empty page.\n\nBinary is explicitly out of scope, and the doc says why: a tool returning bytes\nshould return a presigned URL (storage.PresignGet). Base64 in a tool result\nspends the agent''s context on data it cannot read.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(mcp): ToolDigest — let a module say how its own result compresses\n\nTool results are replayed to the model on later turns (api-platform B9), and a\nlarge one has to shrink somehow. Without a digest the only option is a blind\nbyte truncation — which keeps the FIRST n bytes, and the first n bytes of a\nfifty-row table is rows one to four.\n\nA module knows that \"50 users, first five: …, filter by role to narrow\" answers\nmore later questions in fewer tokens than rows one to four do. Nothing outside\nthe module knows that, which is why this has to be declared rather than\ninferred.\n\nThe digest travels beside the full result on the tool-call response, so the\ncurrent turn still sees everything and only the REPLAY is compressed. It is\ncomputed only above 4 KB — matching the platform''s per-result replay budget, so\na result that already fits needs no stand-in — and it is dropped if it comes\nback larger than what it stands in for.\n\n🔴 **It recovers from a panic, and that is not defensive padding.** The contract\nis that a digest never fails the call, and an error return alone does not\ndeliver that: the obvious digest is a slice expression, and the obvious bug is\n`r.Users[:5]` on a result with three rows. Without the recover, a tool that\nworks perfectly returns 500 the first time its list comes back short.\n\nThat is not hypothetical — the test for this wrote exactly that bug on the\nfirst attempt, panicked, and is the reason the recover exists. It now asserts\nthe panic comes back as a droppable error.\n\nA failed digest degrades to truncation, which is precisely what a module that\ndeclared no digest gets. Fidelity is the only thing at risk, never the tool.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(audit): ms/audit — record what a module did, with no way to say who did it\n\nThe SDK half of the platform-owned audit store (api-platform migration 077).\n\n🔴 **Entry has no actor field, and a test keeps it that way.** Every per-module\naudit table this replaces took the actor from whatever the caller supplied — one\nmodule writes a URL path parameter as the actor on an identity-free internal\nsurface, so its trail records whoever the CALLER SAID it was, which is not an\naudit trail. The platform stamps the actor from the authenticated request\nidentity. A doc comment asking people not to pass an actor is a request; a\nstruct with no such field is a guarantee.\n\n**Record takes the caller''s own transaction and writes to a local outbox.**\nThat is what makes the audit row atomic with the change it describes: a mutation\nthat commits always has its row, and one that rolls back leaves none. Calling\nthe platform inline instead would put a network round trip inside a database\ntransaction and force a choice between losing the audit row and failing the\nmutation — neither acceptable for a record whose whole value is completeness.\nDelivery is therefore at-least-once and may lag.\n\n**The outbox table is Go DDL, not a migration, and it has to be.** An SDK-owned\nnumbered migration can never reach an already-installed module: the runner\nselects migrations above the app''s stored watermark and every shipped module is\nalready past 0001, so a 0000_audit.up.sql sorts below every watermark and is\nskipped forever — giving a two-population schema where the code works in some\ntenants and raises 42P01 in others. The SDK also cannot ship .sql at all,\nbecause the module owns the migration filesystem. So it rides the install\nprovisioner, the same shape the contributions store already uses, advisory lock\nincluded.\n\nThe pending index is PARTIAL (`WHERE delivered_at IS NULL`), so it stays the\nsize of the backlog rather than of the history — a module with a million\ndelivered rows still drains in one seek.\n\nDetails are serialised and size-checked inside Record, so a module handing over\nsomething unserialisable or oversized finds out in its own transaction, where it\ncan still act — not later in a drain, where the only options are dropping the\nrow or retrying it forever.\n\n**TestLifecycleProvisioner_ReadsSlotsPerCall had to change, and that is\nhonest.** It asserted \"no contribution slots means no database work\", which was\ntrue until the outbox joined the ensure step. Contribution storage is only\nneeded by a module that declares slots; the outbox is needed by every module.\nPinning the old assertion would have pinned the outbox out of existence for\nexactly the modules that declare no slots. The per-call slot read it protected\nis still in the code and still commented; it is simply no longer observable\nthrough that seam.\n\nNext: the drain, then the module adoptions (A7, A8).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* release: v0.4.7\n\nCuts the tag user-core needs to adopt httpx.Page and audit.Record. Without\nit those adoptions cannot be pinned: a PR-branch pseudo-version would break\nthe moment this squashes, and a replace directive is not shippable.\n\nAdditive only — 22 exported functions/types added, none removed and none\nchanged in signature, so PATCH per the pre-1.0 convention. Measured with\n`go doc -all` over every non-internal, non-examples package at GOWORK=off,\ndiffed against origin/main: 252 -> 273 unique exported names (21, not 22,\nbecause audit.Record shares a name with the existing ms.Record and the\nname-level diff counts it once).\n\nThe behaviour changes — enforced MCP input schemas, a body cap on every\nroute scope, schemas no longer carrying Go import paths, and an audit\noutbox provisioned for every module at boot — are written up in the\nchangelog entry. None of them alters an exported signature.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8ce1b6e546894f62988bbc7e6d8f80ebb53df4aa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/git/trees/8ce1b6e546894f62988bbc7e6d8f80ebb53df4aa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/git/commits/67541494f18128c69198b2b56feed30f79f246e5","verification":{"payload":"tree 8ce1b6e546894f62988bbc7e6d8f80ebb53df4aa\nparent 0f344c9891f9f66f541480e20beeb4811580f66b\nauthor Sheng Kun Chang <56163905+I-am-nothing@users.noreply.github.com> 1787815141 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787815141 +0800\n\nfix(sdk): nine defects — body caps, MCP schemas, arg validation, annotations, httpx, comment rot (#189)\n\n* fix(core): cap every route''s body, stop leaking import paths into MCP schemas\n\nFour defects that share one shape — a rule written for one case and never\nextended to the sibling case that needed it more.\n\n**The body cap protected the wrong surface.** `scopedRoutes` applied\n`MaxBytes` only when `scope == ScopeInternal`. Internal routes require a shared\nsecret and are reachable only from the platform; Public routes are anonymous\nand are the one surface a stranger can post to. The guarded scope was capped\nand the unguarded one was not — an anonymous POST could stream until the\nmodule ran out of memory. Verified: a 1 MB + 1 byte POST to `/public/*`\nreturned 200 with the whole body read; it is now 413. API Gateway''s 6 MB gave\nproduction a ceiling by accident, but `mirrorstack dev` had none.\n\nNo per-module override, deliberately: nothing in the fleet reads a multipart\nbody or a file upload on any route — bulk bytes go through presigned storage\nURLs and never touch a module route.\n\n**Every MCP tool schema shipped the SDK''s package layout.** `deriveMCPSchema`\nlacked the `Anonymous` its sibling `derivePayloadSchema` has always had, so\neach tool''s inputSchema carried\n\n    \"$id\":\"https://github.com/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/internal/core/args\"\n\nplus a `$schema` dialect line no MCP client reads. A tools/list is re-sent\nevery turn, so both were paid per turn, per tool, forever. The derived schema\ndrops from 245 to 129 bytes on a one-field struct; `type:\"object\"` and the\ninline shape the MCP spec requires are asserted in the test.\n\n**`examples/template` was never compiled.** It is its own Go module, so\n`./...` never reached it — which is how it came to ship a `Config` that omits\n`Slug` entirely, teaching a shape the catalog will not accept. CI now builds it\nagainst this tree via its own `replace`, so an SDK change that breaks the\ndocumented example fails here instead of in a reader''s terminal. The docs\ncalled it \"the working scaffold the CLI pulls from\" in both locales; the CLI\nvendors its own templates and does not read this at all.\n\n**`MCPResource` cannot be reached by anything.** Dispatch answers\n`method not found` to every `resources/*` call — its v1 capability set is tools\nonly, by design — and the SDK advertises no resources capability either.\nAdoption is 0 of 11 modules, which is what a surface with no client looks like.\nMarked `Deprecated:` on both the public and internal declarations, pointing at\nan argument-less `MCPTool` as the reachable equivalent. Not removed: that\nbreaks a published API, and there is no evidence anyone calls it — only\nevidence that it would not work if they did.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): enforce the input schema the module publishes, and name undocumented fields\n\nMCPTool''s doc comment has always promised arguments are \"validated against\nincoming JSON at call time\". Nothing validated anything: the call path ran\njson.Unmarshal, which ignores both constraints the derived schema states.\n\n**`required` was advertised and enforced by nobody.** Go leaves an absent field\nat its zero value, so a tool declaring a required `userId` ran with `userId:\"\"`\n— and if the handler read that as \"all users\" or \"me\", it did the wrong thing\nand said nothing. An LLM omitting an argument is the ordinary case, not the\nexotic one.\n\n**`additionalProperties:false` likewise.** A model calling `{\"user_id\": …}`\nagainst a `userId` field got the silent zero value AND no signal it had\nmisspelled anything. Both now return ErrInvalidArgs, which the MCP layer turns\ninto an in-band error the model can read and retry against.\n\nPresence is checked against the raw object rather than the decoded struct,\nbecause JSON Schema''s `required` is about the KEY: an explicit null satisfies\nit, and a zero value from an absent key does not. DisallowUnknownFields is\nexactly additionalProperties:false here — the schema''s properties are derived\nfrom the struct''s fields, so the two sets are the same at every nesting level.\nThe required list is parsed once at registration, not per call.\n\n**0 of 45 shipped tools documented a single field**, and the reason turned out\nto be that nothing ever mentioned it was possible: `jsonschema:\"description=…\"`\nstruct tags already flow into the derived schema at zero cost. Registration now\nlogs a warning naming every undescribed input field, sorted so the line is\nstable across boots, and the doc comment shows the tag. A warning, not an error\n— failing a module''s boot over prose is a worse trade than shipping it\nundocumented.\n\nNot included: ToolReadOnly/ToolDestructive/ToolIdempotent annotations. The\ndispatch MCP surface carries no annotations field at all, so declaring them\nhere would stop at the manifest and be read by nobody — the same\ndeclared-but-unimplemented shape this commit is removing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(docs): cite symbols in comments, never line numbers\n\nThis repo carried 19 cross-file citations of the form `db.go:103`. Resolving\neach one against the file it names showed **14 of them pointed at the wrong\nthing**: a blank line, a fragment of an unrelated comment, a closing brace, a\nlogger call inside a different function. They had drifted by 1 to 92 lines\nwhile still reading as authoritative.\n\n    cited                          actually lands on\n    call.go:29                     (blank line)            — meant callHTTP, at :63\n    module.go:196                  m.logger.Printf(...)    — meant moduleIDPattern, at :208\n    system/manifest.go:209         return ui               — meant manifestHandler, at :301\n    select.go:98                   }                       — meant queryDynamicSelect\n    module.go:168                  // schema identifier.   — meant devAppSchemaMiddleware, at :180\n\nA reference that is confidently wrong is worse than no reference: it sends the\nnext reader somewhere real and irrelevant. Nothing about editing the cited file\nmakes the citing file''s comment update, so this rots by construction.\n\nAll 19 now name a symbol. `resolvePoolFor (db.go)` costs one grep and cannot\ndrift; `db.go:103` was already three lines stale the day it was written.\n\nA test enforces it repo-wide, in `go test ./...` rather than CI-only so it\nfails before the push. It scans comments in every .go file including tests —\na rotted comment misleads just as well in a test — and it first asserts its own\nmatcher fires on `db.go:103`, `select.go:120-140` and `system/manifest.go:209`\nand does NOT fire on `resolvePoolFor (db.go)`. Without that, a regression\nmaking the pattern match nothing would read as a pass.\n\nVerified by planting `see db.go:103` and watching it fail:\n\n    comment_citations_test.go:92: comment cites a line number:\n      internal/core/db.go:296 cites db.go:103 — name the symbol instead\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(mcp): ToolReadOnly / ToolDestructive / ToolIdempotent / ToolTitle\n\nThe variadic MCPToolOption seam was built for exactly this, and the only reason\nit stayed empty is that there was nowhere for the value to go — nothing between\nthe module and the model carried an annotations field, so declaring these would\nhave produced data read by nobody.\n\napi-platform now carries them end to end (migration 075 → the MCP catalog →\nboth wire shapes → the agent''s tool description), so these are real the day\nthey ship. A tool marked ToolDestructive reaches the model as:\n\n    [DESTRUCTIVE: this changes or removes data irreversibly]\n    Delete a user.\n    Returns: {deleted: boolean}\n\n**Unset is not read-only, and this is the whole design.** The hints are\n*pointers* internally and omitted entirely when undeclared, because absent\nmeans \"the module said nothing\" while false means \"the module said no\" — a\nreader that cannot tell those apart has lost the only thing a hint is for.\nDefaulting readOnly to true would silently vouch for every tool ever written,\nincluding the ones that delete things, which is exactly backwards for a hint\nwhose only job is to let a caller relax.\n\nNothing is emitted at all when no option is given: an empty `{}` would read\ndownstream as a module that considered the question and declined, which is a\ndifferent claim from never having been asked.\n\nThe object is marshalled from a map, so keys are sorted and the manifest bytes\nare stable across boots — a manifest that reshuffled would look like a content\nchange to every consumer that hashes it. A test pins that two callers passing\nthe same options in different order produce identical bytes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(httpx): one pagination shape for every module\n\nAcross the shipped modules there were three pagination dialects and two\nBYTE-IDENTICAL cursor functions written independently in different repos:\ncredit''s `auditCursor` and users-roles'' `roleAuditCursor` are the same function\ncharacter for character, and user-core runs a third scheme on top. Nothing was\nwrong with any one of them — there was simply no owner for the convention, so\nevery module invented it again. This package is that owner.\n\n**Keyset always, offset never.** There is no Offset here and there will not be\none. Offset pagination is wrong under concurrent writes in a way that never\nshows up in testing: a row inserted before the cursor position shifts every\nlater row down, so page 2 re-shows a row from page 1, and a delete makes page 2\nskip one. Audit logs and ledgers — most of what modules paginate — are exactly\nthe append-heavy tables where that happens constantly.\n\n**The cursor is opaque**, base64url over an internal form. A cursor a client\ncan read is a cursor a client will parse, and then the encoding cannot change\nwithout breaking them.\n\nThree decisions the tests pin, because each was a real bug in one of the\ndialects being replaced:\n\n  - the (time, id) cursor keeps NANOSECONDS. A second-resolution cursor on a\n    table that writes faster than that loses rows at every page boundary, and\n    the id half is the tiebreak that makes the keyset total — two rows written\n    in the same instant otherwise make a boundary ambiguous.\n  - the separator is 0x1f, not ''|''. It cannot occur in an RFC3339 timestamp or\n    a UUID, so a value carrying one cannot split the cursor in the wrong place.\n  - an undecodable cursor is the FIRST page, not a 400. Same answer as no\n    cursor, cannot leak whether some other row exists, and a stale cursor from\n    an old client degrades to \"start over\" rather than an error nobody can act\n    on.\n\nPage[T] marshals an empty page as `{\"items\":[]}` and never null, and\nNextCursor is nil rather than \"\" on the last page — so \"no more\" and \"a\nboundary that encoded as empty\" cannot be confused. Truncated/Note describe a\npage whose ITEMS were cut, which is a different fact from having successors;\nboth can be true at once.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: write down the module skeleton all 11 modules already use\n\nThe rule existed and was universally followed — every shipped module has both\n`declare/` and `internal/handlers/` — but it was written down in exactly one\nplace: one module''s own CLAUDE.md. A new author had to reverse-engineer it from\na neighbour, and `mirrorstack module init` scaffolded a different pattern\nentirely (a postInitHooks registry with 0 of 11 adoption).\n\nThe boundary is one question: does this code mount a route or carry an\nhttp.HandlerFunc? No → `declare/`. Yes → `internal/handlers/`.\n\nThat split is not aesthetic. The declaration set BECOMES the manifest, and the\nmanifest is what the platform reads to decide what a module may do — install\npreconditions, permission defaults, exposed tables, contribution slots. Keeping\nit in files that cannot see a request is what makes it a statement about the\nmodule rather than about one caller.\n\nTwo rules recorded because both were learned the hard way:\n\n  - main.go never grows. Two Register() entry points, not a pile of init()\n    functions — an init() registry looks tidier and costs deterministic\n    ordering, and i18n catalogs MUST load before permission labels resolve.\n  - never name a file handlers.go. It is the name you reach for when you have\n    not decided what the code is, and it is how one module''s handlers.go\n    reached 1038 lines holding sessions, admin, profile and metering at once.\n\nBoth locales, linked from both READMEs. This is what the CLI scaffold should\nproduce (D6) — it does not yet.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sdk): unblock the two things standing in front of any latency fix\n\nBoth are one-line changes that do nothing on their own and are load-bearing the\nmoment anyone parallelises a module''s contribution fan-out.\n\n**cache.ForApp panicked on every real app id.** Its idPattern was\n`^[a-z0-9_]+$` — no hyphens — but app ids are UUIDs. runtime.AppSchemaName\nexists precisely because of it, rewriting ''-'' to ''_'' before the value can be a\nPostgres identifier; nothing did the equivalent here. So the first module to\ncall ms.Cache().ForApp(ms.AppID(ctx), …) crashed on its first real request, and\nthe unit test passed because it used a hand-written \"app_abc123\" no caller\nwould ever produce.\n\nThe only thing that pattern has to stop is a COLON — keys are\n{appID}:{moduleID}:{key}, so an id carrying '':'' could climb out of its own\nnamespace and read another app''s data. A hyphen cannot break a prefix boundary.\nExcluding it bought nothing and cost the whole cache. Colons, spaces and\nuppercase still panic, and a test pins that.\n\n**callHTTP inherited DefaultTransport''s 2-connection idle pool.** Every\ninter-module call from a module goes to the SAME host (dispatch). While a\nfan-out is serial that is invisible — concurrency never exceeds 1. The moment a\nhost module fans out to N contributors in parallel, connections 3..N cannot be\npooled: each pays a fresh TCP handshake (plus TLS in production) and is CLOSED\nrather than returned, on every request. Most of the parallel win goes straight\nback into handshakes, and it presents as \"parallelising didn''t help much\"\nrather than as a misconfigured pool.\n\nNow an explicit transport CLONED from DefaultTransport — proxy handling,\ntimeouts and HTTP/2 unchanged, only the pool sizes raised. A nil clone (if\nsomething replaced DefaultTransport) falls back to the old behaviour rather\nthan guessing.\n\nThe test asserts it here because the symptom appears in a different repo: the\nmodule doing the fan-out pays for it, the SDK causes it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(core): split the SDK''s own 1069-line module.go\n\nmodule.go held config validation, construction, all three route scopes, the\npermission system, startup and shutdown, the reserved /__mirrorstack/* route\ntree, the package-level default module, and contribution slots.\n\nIt is the SDK''s own version of the catch-all this codebase now tells module\nauthors not to write — the same shape as the user-core handlers.go split in\nms-app-modules #265, in the repo that documents the rule.\n\n    module.go                  141   construction, and the accessors others reach through\n    module_config.go           225   declared identity + construction-time validation\n    module_scopes.go           148   the three scopes and each one''s middleware chain\n    module_permissions.go      186   declaration, qualification, enforcement, i18n labels\n    module_start.go            180   serving mode, the one-shot task path, Close\n    module_system_routes.go    148   the reserved /__mirrorstack/* namespace\n    module_default.go           57   the default module and the ms.* wrappers\n    module_contributions.go    127   declaring slots, reading what others registered\n\nPure motion — no logic changed, `go test -race ./...` passes unchanged, and\nimports were pruned per file by goimports rather than by hand.\n\nThe `module_` prefix rather than bare names (config.go, start.go) because this\npackage already has 30 files and a bare `start.go` would read as the package''s\nstartup rather than the Module type''s.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct the declare/ rule — event and cron handlers are a real exception\n\nThe rule as written said nothing in declare/ should reference an *http.Request.\nWriting the enforcement script disproved it: ms.OnEvent and ms.Cron take a\nhandler with an HTTP shape, and every module using them keeps that handler in\ndeclare/ beside its subscription. Moving it would need declare/ to import\nhandlers, which already imports declare — so the honest options are an import\ncycle or a third package, and the fleet had settled this correctly.\n\nFlagging those was a false positive on a pattern that was already right. What\ndeclare/ must never do is MOUNT A ROUTE, and that is what\nscripts/verify-module.sh now checks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(httpx): Cap — bound a tool result by dropping whole items, never bytes\n\n🔴 The obvious implementation is the broken one. Marshalling a result and\ncutting the string is how most size caps get written, and it produces INVALID\nJSON: the reader gets a parse error instead of data, or — worse — a\nhalf-written object it silently mis-reads. Every test here asserts the result\nstill parses, at every size, including when nothing fit.\n\nCap drops whole items until the marshalled slice fits, and returns a page that\nSAYS SO: Truncated marks it, Note gives the count and how to recover the rest,\nand NextCursor is the position to resume from. A model reading it asks for the\nnext page instead of concluding the list is short — which is exactly what a\nsilently-shortened list teaches it to do.\n\nAn empty result is marked truncated too, rather than being an empty list the\ncaller reads as \"there is nothing\".\n\nIt halves rather than steps, so a single oversized item costs log(n) marshals\ninstead of n marshals of an already-too-large slice. An unmarshalable item is\nreturned as-is: that is the caller''s bug, and failing where it can be diagnosed\nbeats silently returning an empty page.\n\nBinary is explicitly out of scope, and the doc says why: a tool returning bytes\nshould return a presigned URL (storage.PresignGet). Base64 in a tool result\nspends the agent''s context on data it cannot read.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(mcp): ToolDigest — let a module say how its own result compresses\n\nTool results are replayed to the model on later turns (api-platform B9), and a\nlarge one has to shrink somehow. Without a digest the only option is a blind\nbyte truncation — which keeps the FIRST n bytes, and the first n bytes of a\nfifty-row table is rows one to four.\n\nA module knows that \"50 users, first five: …, filter by role to narrow\" answers\nmore later questions in fewer tokens than rows one to four do. Nothing outside\nthe module knows that, which is why this has to be declared rather than\ninferred.\n\nThe digest travels beside the full result on the tool-call response, so the\ncurrent turn still sees everything and only the REPLAY is compressed. It is\ncomputed only above 4 KB — matching the platform''s per-result replay budget, so\na result that already fits needs no stand-in — and it is dropped if it comes\nback larger than what it stands in for.\n\n🔴 **It recovers from a panic, and that is not defensive padding.** The contract\nis that a digest never fails the call, and an error return alone does not\ndeliver that: the obvious digest is a slice expression, and the obvious bug is\n`r.Users[:5]` on a result with three rows. Without the recover, a tool that\nworks perfectly returns 500 the first time its list comes back short.\n\nThat is not hypothetical — the test for this wrote exactly that bug on the\nfirst attempt, panicked, and is the reason the recover exists. It now asserts\nthe panic comes back as a droppable error.\n\nA failed digest degrades to truncation, which is precisely what a module that\ndeclared no digest gets. Fidelity is the only thing at risk, never the tool.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(audit): ms/audit — record what a module did, with no way to say who did it\n\nThe SDK half of the platform-owned audit store (api-platform migration 077).\n\n🔴 **Entry has no actor field, and a test keeps it that way.** Every per-module\naudit table this replaces took the actor from whatever the caller supplied — one\nmodule writes a URL path parameter as the actor on an identity-free internal\nsurface, so its trail records whoever the CALLER SAID it was, which is not an\naudit trail. The platform stamps the actor from the authenticated request\nidentity. A doc comment asking people not to pass an actor is a request; a\nstruct with no such field is a guarantee.\n\n**Record takes the caller''s own transaction and writes to a local outbox.**\nThat is what makes the audit row atomic with the change it describes: a mutation\nthat commits always has its row, and one that rolls back leaves none. Calling\nthe platform inline instead would put a network round trip inside a database\ntransaction and force a choice between losing the audit row and failing the\nmutation — neither acceptable for a record whose whole value is completeness.\nDelivery is therefore at-least-once and may lag.\n\n**The outbox table is Go DDL, not a migration, and it has to be.** An SDK-owned\nnumbered migration can never reach an already-installed module: the runner\nselects migrations above the app''s stored watermark and every shipped module is\nalready past 0001, so a 0000_audit.up.sql sorts below every watermark and is\nskipped forever — giving a two-population schema where the code works in some\ntenants and raises 42P01 in others. The SDK also cannot ship .sql at all,\nbecause the module owns the migration filesystem. So it rides the install\nprovisioner, the same shape the contributions store already uses, advisory lock\nincluded.\n\nThe pending index is PARTIAL (`WHERE delivered_at IS NULL`), so it stays the\nsize of the backlog rather than of the history — a module with a million\ndelivered rows still drains in one seek.\n\nDetails are serialised and size-checked inside Record, so a module handing over\nsomething unserialisable or oversized finds out in its own transaction, where it\ncan still act — not later in a drain, where the only options are dropping the\nrow or retrying it forever.\n\n**TestLifecycleProvisioner_ReadsSlotsPerCall had to change, and that is\nhonest.** It asserted \"no contribution slots means no database work\", which was\ntrue until the outbox joined the ensure step. Contribution storage is only\nneeded by a module that declares slots; the outbox is needed by every module.\nPinning the old assertion would have pinned the outbox out of existence for\nexactly the modules that declare no slots. The per-call slot read it protected\nis still in the code and still commented; it is simply no longer observable\nthrough that seam.\n\nNext: the drain, then the module adoptions (A7, A8).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* release: v0.4.7\n\nCuts the tag user-core needs to adopt httpx.Page and audit.Record. Without\nit those adoptions cannot be pinned: a PR-branch pseudo-version would break\nthe moment this squashes, and a replace directive is not shippable.\n\nAdditive only — 22 exported functions/types added, none removed and none\nchanged in signature, so PATCH per the pre-1.0 convention. Measured with\n`go doc -all` over every non-internal, non-examples package at GOWORK=off,\ndiffed against origin/main: 252 -> 273 unique exported names (21, not 22,\nbecause audit.Record shares a name with the existing ms.Record and the\nname-level diff counts it once).\n\nThe behaviour changes — enforced MCP input schemas, a body cap on every\nroute scope, schemas no longer carrying Go import paths, and an audit\noutbox provisioned for every module at boot — are written up in the\nchangelog entry. None of them alters an exported signature.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqj+TmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAALvMQAHic2HYW2vglvFrHZW2Ych+n\n/UWzcR185KVR7kdmj95KEmwjjkq7Uzh8l7eoh7S8CJYATOuYY0H3J6QjmtLzfqnK\n/zmjMFfb5f6v+BpANXRsMHYF85JpUDcB7SRPKHHY/3gd5bLIbhkvmoh5dYHrJ2x6\n53V51sp2/d92vYAhCL4YZFvB9J3yZhJyey4SjonOgfs0Ojipr95Wy4uZIscoX31c\nyy/ECEG08FkeI2H5SgH/YRyw1iq5FwA6nLx4/mSwIgU6P6HtwKJq1ui/MFp31tCL\nkJfIuite8abEft/ZppJgzlXqSXsbP2ePU7EJbuzjLUbaJPGKoH0AneSE6n6R/5XE\n4ReFkJ8kks6765frzSo+iyBZx7ejQW4ciDg29zgg7+wHdCUiwyjv8YgPbMm2Kt4x\n0OP/TifF63qQc8BmPIkee3VmAlmUz4G23jmB89v3jYoCbh5nzacNez4UexCYTozW\nmgJYnarZ81kwlkQO7OtyQOtLVVND3fPiK50JhNMIcExazbQeSBcVbeRNRbZmdeXP\nDXW5uQmfolK2lcOGRCAXHxXOaLwTcqbG4SDkh6ik7uFUZs2L6rdZYj9o7M3ztdvv\nShHDiyJlKge5runB5eBjejSwMDvlKzHoXuuzJNdy60tvnWjMh789Yop0VTC2DUuU\nr59r2MRtFjpb1Z7TCsPL\n=fJXh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-27T07:19:02Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commit/67541494f18128c69198b2b56feed30f79f246e5","node_id":"C_kwDOR2zucNoAKDY3NTQxNDk0ZjE4MTI4YzY5MTk4YjJiNTZmZWVkMzBmNzlmMjQ2ZTU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commit/0f344c9891f9f66f541480e20beeb4811580f66b","sha":"0f344c9891f9f66f541480e20beeb4811580f66b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commits/0f344c9891f9f66f541480e20beeb4811580f66b"}],"sha":"67541494f18128c69198b2b56feed30f79f246e5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commits/67541494f18128c69198b2b56feed30f79f246e5"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/56163905?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/I-am-nothing","id":56163905,"login":"I-am-nothing","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2MTYzOTA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commits/0f344c9891f9f66f541480e20beeb4811580f66b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T03:07:30Z","email":"56163905+I-am-nothing@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Sheng Kun Chang"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T03:07:30Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(meter): ms.AbsorbInfra() — zero every platform infra metric without naming one (#188)\n\n* feat(meter): ms.AbsorbInfra() — zero every platform infra metric without naming one\n\nA module that bills its customer through its own meters usually wants to add no\ninfrastructure passthrough on top. The only way to say that was one\nms.Meter(\"infra.X\", ms.Price(0)) per metric — a copy, in module code, of a\ncatalog the module does not own.\n\nThat copy has already gone stale. This SDK''s own documented example named\ninfra.compute.ms; the platform re-chartered that metric to\ninfra.compute.walltime.ms (billing migration 019) and then deleted the alias\n(022). Every module following the example has since been declaring a price for a\nmetric that does not exist — and because the platform validates the override\nbatch all-or-nothing and only logs the rejection, that one stale name silently\ntook the module''s OTHER overrides down with it.\n\nms.AbsorbInfra() is one manifest boolean. The platform expands it against its own\ncatalog at publish, so a metric introduced next quarter is absorbed with no\nrepublish, and there is no second list anywhere to keep in step. An explicit\nms.Meter(\"infra.X\", ms.Price(n)) is applied after the absorb and wins:\n\n\tms.AbsorbInfra()                                       // baseline: all infra at 0\n\tms.Meter(\"infra.egress.api.bytes\", ms.Price(120_000))  // …except egress\n\nAlso corrects infra.compute.ms → infra.compute.walltime.ms everywhere it is\nshown, and points the reader at AbsorbInfra with the reason.\n\n* chore: release v0.4.6\n\n* docs(changelog): state how the v0.4.6 symbol count was measured","tree":{"sha":"a8949e5e24ba5882a66bd93e55b6d7b943cf4e08","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/git/trees/a8949e5e24ba5882a66bd93e55b6d7b943cf4e08"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/git/commits/0f344c9891f9f66f541480e20beeb4811580f66b","verification":{"payload":"tree a8949e5e24ba5882a66bd93e55b6d7b943cf4e08\nparent 7231b56628c8f5c5b84b30e7a13b662d70b4aaea\nauthor Sheng Kun Chang <56163905+I-am-nothing@users.noreply.github.com> 1787713650 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787713650 +0800\n\nfeat(meter): ms.AbsorbInfra() — zero every platform infra metric without naming one (#188)\n\n* feat(meter): ms.AbsorbInfra() — zero every platform infra metric without naming one\n\nA module that bills its customer through its own meters usually wants to add no\ninfrastructure passthrough on top. The only way to say that was one\nms.Meter(\"infra.X\", ms.Price(0)) per metric — a copy, in module code, of a\ncatalog the module does not own.\n\nThat copy has already gone stale. This SDK''s own documented example named\ninfra.compute.ms; the platform re-chartered that metric to\ninfra.compute.walltime.ms (billing migration 019) and then deleted the alias\n(022). Every module following the example has since been declaring a price for a\nmetric that does not exist — and because the platform validates the override\nbatch all-or-nothing and only logs the rejection, that one stale name silently\ntook the module''s OTHER overrides down with it.\n\nms.AbsorbInfra() is one manifest boolean. The platform expands it against its own\ncatalog at publish, so a metric introduced next quarter is absorbed with no\nrepublish, and there is no second list anywhere to keep in step. An explicit\nms.Meter(\"infra.X\", ms.Price(n)) is applied after the absorb and wins:\n\n\tms.AbsorbInfra()                                       // baseline: all infra at 0\n\tms.Meter(\"infra.egress.api.bytes\", ms.Price(120_000))  // …except egress\n\nAlso corrects infra.compute.ms → infra.compute.walltime.ms everywhere it is\nshown, and points the reader at AbsorbInfra with the reason.\n\n* chore: release v0.4.6\n\n* docs(changelog): state how the v0.4.6 symbol count was measured","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjlhyCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAN1MQAIJCF9Zytt3SOzxOZnKkMuye\n41UqbOr9Gc4tD/OPOaQ6VX/N5VExSm/8wFHlAQJp1jWp6idhNoQcvxBWqNvWZ7aU\nj6CnBxRrpJ6nnGWTokwQTiw9hjmJZ6WXTKzAZMe/pSYFc/4EN+VxFJ0u3+0fVWHC\nYffZYq1dC1p8Nhr2zYS3zIt9Q4mAGuaNNyxpfG3uDYxRQwRtRyy5TQgtvUFZcRcN\nAuo9MT0Yiq3gg1NDSb0vWZVxHVxUifAL1Bujd3O/swO5bkTo3LxAsQllns26Jk6j\nwMwiLArjEE7iZHjRJrW1S9gjXGHo3Gy7A6Xzb4rMuPkacrk6aWd1mJBOs6QO0Tzg\nHIjDBCSqAMR6WO2PXcd2DO8J4IzO5HnE9K6KZSK7BrntWvxCNJ67Jt+wZjeJMeJD\nxyt3aOm3VcKAkTP6P4MpOAVPYYerZVlBhf7wjdj+43/0Er9qNSQAaGTIraNFMvxt\nsfYWyrHecYhyBWFV4apzyPrFPFFsato8l1HNIlB/V4TpNHzB+U4R3/aeFZJ5guZ4\nMStB0kGRDhCDwyPIrsbtxyCXOsdFJg+DftqL8AGAXDcqH8MZKScxrkxYo6DC8YWP\n1ytUowdg3f4VFyBprmmYwRZWsz83yBGjPt/SYOv8V/4QICNAY/jydudpby7qJHyi\naXY/ua3JpoxufdYFDZmA\n=12e+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-26T03:07:31Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/f