,"verified_at":"2026-08-16T19:10:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commit/15b24b44bdc992f3cafc3d9bf63fa6feb66239da","node_id":"C_kwDOR2zucNoAKDE1YjI0YjQ0YmRjOTkyZjNjYWZjM2Q5YmY2M2ZhNmZlYjY2MjM5ZGE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commit/f615fe5f37088cc7ba54ec610be99094487a4ecc","sha":"f615fe5f37088cc7ba54ec610be99094487a4ecc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commits/f615fe5f37088cc7ba54ec610be99094487a4ecc"}],"sha":"15b24b44bdc992f3cafc3d9bf63fa6feb66239da","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commits/15b24b44bdc992f3cafc3d9bf63fa6feb66239da"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/56163905?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/I-am-nothing","id":56163905,"login":"I-am-nothing","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2MTYzOTA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/I-am-nothing","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/commits/f615fe5f37088cc7ba54ec610be99094487a4ecc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-16T18:46:50Z","email":"56163905+I-am-nothing@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Sheng Kun Chang"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-16T18:46:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(auth): mint a platform-notarised member assertion (#183)\n\n* feat(identity): mint platform-signed member assertions (ms.MintMemberAssertion)\n\nPR 2 of #518. The auth-provider module asserts identity; the platform\nnotarises it. This is the module-facing half.\n\nDispatch builds a caller with literally zero identity for every\nnon-platform, non-internal path, so a module''s /public/* handler has never\nbeen able to see who is asking. An entitlement gate reading userID == \"\"\ndenies a signed-in member correctly and uselessly: the gate is right, there\nis simply nobody standing behind it. MintMemberAssertion is how somebody\ngets there.\n\nWhy the platform notarises rather than each module resolving a session for\nitself: the latter breaks the audit rule (actor from ctx ONLY) — an actor a\nmodule fetched for itself is not one the platform can guarantee, so the\nfirst module that forgets writes a row claiming a system actor for a human\naction. It also hands every module a credential that can act AS that member\nlater, for anything. Notarising keeps ms.UserID(ctx) uniformly true and\nkeeps the member''s own credential inside the module that owns members.\n\nBuilt on the exact rail ms.Delivery uses — appIDFromContext,\noutboundServiceSecret, postDispatchJSONInto against an app-scoped dispatch\nURL with X-MS-Service-Secret as an opt-in header. Package-level func, not\nan interface method: by the Storer/PrefixDeleter rule a method goes on an\ninterface only when it is fundamental to what every holder does, and this\nabstracts over nothing — there is exactly one platform. An interface here\nwould invent a seam whose only purpose is to let something other than the\nplatform mint identity, which is the one thing the design prevents. The\nreasoning is written down in the doc comment, not just in this message.\n\nFails closed, and here the degraded return is the dangerous one: an empty\ntoken on a request is indistinguishable at the far end from no token, so\nevery downstream module would see an ANONYMOUS caller while the auth module\nbelieved it signed someone in. Empty token, non-positive lifetime, or a\ntoken that is not header-safe (reusing actor.ValidTransportValue, since it\nwill ride Authorization: Bearer) are all errors returning the zero value.\nThe token''s FORMAT is deliberately not pinned — dispatch owns it, and\nasserting msm1 would make a future msm2 a breaking SDK change.\n\nUnlike DeliveryTicket the credential is exported, because handing the string\nonward is the whole point. That costs the second fail-closed layer, so the\nresponse screening is pinned by its own test rather than only through the\nmint path.\n\nAlso renames deliveryTTLSeconds -> dispatchTTLSeconds and moves it to\ndispatch_transport.go. Internal only; it is now shared by two mint surfaces\nand should not live in one surface''s file under one surface''s name.\n\nEvery security-relevant test was mutation-proved. Two mutations initially\nSURVIVED and each bought a real test:\n  - returning the token alongside a transport error was invisible, because\n    no case made encoding/json partially populate the struct before\n    erroring. Added a 200 whose expiresIn is a string, which does exactly\n    that and leaves a token behind.\n  - dispatchBaseFor(ctx) -> dispatchBase() was invisible, so a task\n    attempt''s broker-attested routing could be silently ignored. Added a\n    test that ctx routing beats MS_DISPATCH_URL. (Delivery has the same\n    blind spot; not fixed here.)\nThe explicit empty-token check was also invisible, since ValidTransportValue\nrejects \"\" too; kept it — an empty response is a different bug from a\nmalformed one and must not depend on another package''s rule staying strict —\nand pinned it by asserting the error wording.\n\nINERT UNTIL PR 1 SHIPS. POST /apps/{appID}/member-assertions exists in no\ndeployed platform; until then every call errors, which is the correct\nsequencing. The tests fake dispatch with httptest, so they prove nothing\nabout the real endpoint: the wire contract must be diffed field-by-field\nagainst the platform handler''s source before either side merges.\n\nNo VERSION bump — the release is a separate `release`-labelled PR.\n\nRefs #518\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(auth): pin the member-assertion wire contract and fail closed on a hollow lifetime\n\nReview follow-up on the #518 SDK half. Three findings had PROVEN mutations\nthat survived the suite; each is fixed and each fix is mutation-verified.\n\n1. THE WIRE CONTRACT WAS ASSERTED AGAINST THE CODE''S OWN STRUCT TAGS.\n   The tests decoded the recorded request into memberAssertionRequest and\n   encoded the fake response from memberAssertionResponse — the same structs\n   production uses — so a JSON tag rename moved both sides at once and no\n   assertion could see it. The envelope version was worse than tautological:\n   it compared the constant to itself. Six mutations SURVIVED the whole suite\n   (v 1->2; userId->user_id; v->version; token->assertion; expiresIn->ttl;\n   expiresAt->exp), and each of them is a 100% mint failure in production\n   against a green SDK.\n\n   Now: the request''s `v` and `userId` are asserted on the RAW JSON against\n   LITERALS, and a new test serves the platform handler''s literal response\n   body — copied field-for-field from its source, not generated from this\n   package''s types — so token/expiresAt/expiresIn are pinned by bytes. All\n   six mutations now FAIL.\n\n2. THE ATTEMPT-SCOPED TASK CREDENTIAL PATH WAS DEAD TO THE SUITE.\n   outboundServiceSecret exists so a denied or expired broker capability is\n   an ERROR and never a fallback to ambient MS_INTERNAL_SECRET. No test\n   installed a capability provider, so collapsing the call to\n   moduleSessionSecret() left the package green — a revoked attempt would go\n   on minting identity assertions naming arbitrary members under a\n   process-wide secret the broker had already declined to renew. This is the\n   exact sibling of the dispatchBaseFor blind spot already covered for\n   routing; routing got a test, the credential did not.\n\n   Added: the attempt-scoped token is what goes on the wire over an ambient\n   secret, plus three fail-closed cases (refresh denied, empty token,\n   expired) each asserting zero requests made. The collapse now FAILS.\n\n3. A HOLLOW 200 COULD STILL PRODUCE A LIVE-LOOKING ASSERTION.\n   `resp.ExpiresIn <= 0` screened the wire integer, but the caller receives\n   time.Duration(seconds), which overflows above ~9.2e9 seconds and wraps\n   NEGATIVE — precisely what a platform returning milliseconds by mistake\n   produces. Measured: expiresIn=17179869184 returned a nil error and\n   ExpiresIn=-351909h, an ExpiresAt already in the past. Since the type''s\n   contract is \"renew off ExpiresIn, not your own proposal\", that is either a\n   renewal storm or a deadline behind us. Now the computed Duration is\n   screened, with a 24h sanity ceiling above it for values that are absurd\n   without overflowing — deliberately far above the platform''s 5-minute clamp,\n   because the SDK must not enforce a policy dispatch owns.\n\nAlso:\n- The happy-path test proposed 5m and the fake returned 300s, so its comment\n  (\"the CLAMPED value the platform granted\") named a property the assertion\n  could not see: an implementation echoing the caller''s proposal passed\n  identically. It now proposes 1h against a 300s grant.\n- CHANGELOG: \"2 added lines, 0 removed\" did not reconcile when re-run —\n  `go doc -all` prints each symbol''s full doc comment, so the literal diff is\n  36 added lines containing 2 added symbols. Restated so the one claim framed\n  as a measurement actually measures.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a96652bfcbcc04a634262926f4eb562d3f2f2807","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/git/trees/a96652bfcbcc04a634262926f4eb562d3f2f2807"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mirrorstack-ai/app-module-sdk/git/commits/f615fe5f37088cc7ba54ec610be99094487a4ecc","verification":{"payload":"tree a96652bfcbcc04a634262926f4eb562d3f2f2807\nparent c4bdc96c309870ffba735473102dbc5e7157b0ad\nauthor Sheng Kun Chang <56163905+I-am-nothing@users.noreply.github.com> 1786906010 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786906010 +0800\n\nfeat(auth): mint a platform-notarised member assertion (#183)\n\n* feat(identity): mint platform-signed member assertions (ms.MintMemberAssertion)\n\nPR 2 of #518. The auth-provider module asserts identity; the platform\nnotarises it. This is the module-facing half.\n\nDispatch builds a caller with literally zero identity for every\nnon-platform, non-internal path, so a module''s /public/* handler has never\nbeen able to see who is asking. An entitlement gate reading userID == \"\"\ndenies a signed-in member correctly and uselessly: the gate is right, there\nis simply nobody standing behind it. MintMemberAssertion is how somebody\ngets there.\n\nWhy the platform notarises rather than each module resolving a session for\nitself: the latter breaks the audit rule (actor from ctx ONLY) — an actor a\nmodule fetched for itself is not one the platform can guarantee, so the\nfirst module that forgets writes a row claiming a system actor for a human\naction. It also hands every module a credential that can act AS that member\nlater, for anything. Notarising keeps ms.UserID(ctx) uniformly true and\nkeeps the member''s own credential inside the module that owns members.\n\nBuilt on the exact rail ms.Delivery uses — appIDFromContext,\noutboundServiceSecret, postDispatchJSONInto against an app-scoped dispatch\nURL with X-MS-Service-Secret as an opt-in header. Package-level func, not\nan interface method: by the Storer/PrefixDeleter rule a method goes on an\ninterface only when it is fundamental to what every holder does, and this\nabstracts over nothing — there is exactly one platform. An interface here\nwould invent a seam whose only purpose is to let something other than the\nplatform mint identity, which is the one thing the design prevents. The\nreasoning is written down in the doc comment, not just in this message.\n\nFails closed, and here the degraded return is the dangerous one: an empty\ntoken on a request is indistinguishable at the far end from no token, so\nevery downstream module would see an ANONYMOUS caller while the auth module\nbelieved it signed someone in. Empty token, non-positive lifetime, or a\ntoken that is not header-safe (reusing actor.ValidTransportValue, since it\nwill ride Authorization: Bearer) are all errors returning the zero value.\nThe token''s FORMAT is deliberately not pinned — dispatch owns it, and\nasserting msm1 would make a future msm2 a breaking SDK change.\n\nUnlike DeliveryTicket the credential is exported, because handing the string\nonward is the whole point. That costs the second fail-closed layer, so the\nresponse screening is pinned by its own test rather than only through the\nmint path.\n\nAlso renames deliveryTTLSeconds -> dispatchTTLSeconds and moves it to\ndispatch_transport.go. Internal only; it is now shared by two mint surfaces\nand should not live in one surface''s file under one surface''s name.\n\nEvery security-relevant test was mutation-proved. Two mutations initially\nSURVIVED and each bought a real test:\n  - returning the token alongside a transport error was invisible, because\n    no case made encoding/json partially populate the struct before\n    erroring. Added a 200 whose expiresIn is a string, which does exactly\n    that and leaves a token behind.\n  - dispatchBaseFor(ctx) -> dispatchBase() was invisible, so a task\n    attempt''s broker-attested routing could be silently ignored. Added a\n    test that ctx routing beats MS_DISPATCH_URL. (Delivery has the same\n    blind spot; not fixed here.)\nThe explicit empty-token check was also invisible, since ValidTransportValue\nrejects \"\" too; kept it — an empty response is a different bug from a\nmalformed one and must not depend on another package''s rule staying strict —\nand pinned it by asserting the error wording.\n\nINERT UNTIL PR 1 SHIPS. POST /apps/{appID}/member-assertions exists in no\ndeployed platform; until then every call errors, which is the correct\nsequencing. The tests fake dispatch with httptest, so they prove nothing\nabout the real endpoint: the wire contract must be diffed field-by-field\nagainst the platform handler''s source before either side merges.\n\nNo VERSION bump — the release is a separate `release`-labelled PR.\n\nRefs #518\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(auth): pin the member-assertion wire contract and fail closed on a hollow lifetime\n\nReview follow-up on the #518 SDK half. Three findings had PROVEN mutations\nthat survived the suite; each is fixed and each fix is mutation-verified.\n\n1. THE WIRE CONTRACT WAS ASSERTED AGAINST THE CODE''S OWN STRUCT TAGS.\n   The tests decoded the recorded request into memberAssertionRequest and\n   encoded the fake response from memberAssertionResponse — the same structs\n   production uses — so a JSON tag rename moved both sides at once and no\n   assertion could see it. The envelope version was worse than tautological:\n   it compared the constant to itself. Six mutations SURVIVED the whole suite\n   (v 1->2; userId->user_id; v->version; token->assertion; expiresIn->ttl;\n   expiresAt->exp), and each of them is a 100% mint failure in production\n   against a green SDK.\n\n   Now: the request''s `v` and `userId` are asserted on the RAW JSON against\n   LITERALS, and a new test serves the platform handler''s literal response\n   body — copied field-for-field from its source, not generated from this\n   package''s types — so token/expiresAt/expiresIn are pinned by bytes. All\n   six mutations now FAIL.\n\n2. THE ATTEMPT-SCOPED TASK CREDENTIAL PATH WAS DEAD TO THE SUITE.\n   outboundServiceSecret exists so a denied or expired broker capability is\n   an ERROR and never a fallback to ambient MS_INTERNAL_SECRET. No test\n   installed a capability provider, so collapsing the call to\n   moduleSessionSecret() left the package green — a revoked attempt would go\n   on minting identity assertions naming arbitrary members under a\n   process-wide secret the broker had already declined to renew. This is the\n   exact sibling of the dispatchBaseFor blind spot already covered for\n   routing; routing got a test, the credential did not.\n\n   Added: the attempt-scoped token is what goes on the wire over an ambient\n   secret, plus three fail-closed cases (refresh denied, empty token,\n   expired) each asserting zero requests made. The collapse now FAILS.\n\n3. A HOLLOW 200 COULD STILL PRODUCE A LIVE-LOOKING ASSERTION.\n   `resp.ExpiresIn <= 0` screened the wire integer, but the caller receives\n   time.Duration(seconds), which overflows above ~9.2e9 seconds and wraps\n   NEGATIVE — precisely what a platform returning milliseconds by mistake\n   produces. Measured: expiresIn=17179869184 returned a nil error and\n   ExpiresIn=-351909h, an ExpiresAt already in the past. Since the type''s\n   contract is \"renew off ExpiresIn, not your own proposal\", that is either a\n   renewal storm or a deadline behind us. Now the computed Duration is\n   screened, with a 24h sanity ceiling above it for values that are absurd\n   without overflowing — deliberately far above the platform''s 5-minute clamp,\n   because the SDK must not enforce a policy dispatch owns.\n\nAlso:\n- The happy-path test proposed 5m and the fake returned 300s, so its comment\n  (\"the CLAMPED value the platform granted\") named a property the assertion\n  could not see: an implementation echoing the caller''s proposal passed\n  identically. It now proposes 1h against a 300s grant.\n- CHANGELOG: \"2 added lines, 0 removed\" did not reconcile when re-run —\n  `go doc -all` prints each symbol''s full doc comment, so the literal diff is\n  36 added lines containing 2 added symbols. Restated so the one claim framed\n  as a measurement actually measures.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqggWaCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA7H8QAAnqfwvQilb9yqHj4s4VayTY\nRF5U6IOjvPKWWJWGhV9QSCW6kkh3wtb6W14Wuyw5YRqBhcl52vqsXLE0ox+e07SY\n7tNIAbzZOvGGeKMZxkYoDz2DwnU4eVUkykXQNfEhQbG4Qr6d7+UnUfWpY1zZbxnw\n7RN4gryIFhC3CIHq0eT5Y7w1XQJ3NNKBQ0AMZbHs63ucUB7UxoZYGd4C/C0lIH8x\ngH0lJ1zU2B+QekUS0YJMF9ZDAkN7XgoUWfl/wOnsLnZupe1YXj3IVlmyezvbAQfm\nf7FIBF1EdvsmlYwZOJwtqXdDogKOJVtwd+0HwbIzxRU4AOv6XpjTDxLvW1B5OcCY\nB8l9AVzJg8bHLpfs9Y0pSoBTfoNUcZWI4bHVrzYlGc31r3T9nO1EekOYhc/K5vsW\nfEy7ZRDUkUD1LCgUiP65wn2blXMChYkqHcJi8NoLv+fBT+ioebmmdw1EKREBDKYp\n